WEBVTT

00:00:00.680 --> 00:00:03.672
Blur and sharpen look like local operations:

00:00:03.692 --> 00:00:07.135
average the neighbours, or exaggerate their
differences.

00:00:07.155 --> 00:00:08.560
But there's another view,

00:00:08.580 --> 00:00:12.430
where both are just volume knobs on waves of
different speeds.

00:00:12.845 --> 00:00:17.550
Start with one row of pixels crossing an edge:
dark, then suddenly bright.

00:00:17.570 --> 00:00:20.220
Can we build that step out of smooth waves?

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:22.392
Begin with the average brightness.

00:00:22.412 --> 00:00:26.092
Add one slow wave: dark left, bright right,
but soft.

00:00:26.112 --> 00:00:31.118
Add a wave three times as fast and a third as
tall, then five times, then seven.

00:00:31.138 --> 00:00:33.050
Each one sharpens the jump.

00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:38.930
With fifty waves, the sum hugs the step, apart
from small ripples beside the jump.

00:00:38.950 --> 00:00:43.800
So blurring means turning the fast waves down,
and sharpening means turning them up.

00:00:44.365 --> 00:00:46.982
Images have waves in every direction.

00:00:47.002 --> 00:00:51.307
A slow stripe pattern shows up as two dots
near the centre of the spectrum.

00:00:51.327 --> 00:00:53.632
Make it faster: the dots move out.

00:00:53.652 --> 00:00:55.740
Turn it: they turn too.

00:00:56.125 --> 00:01:01.280
A photo is a sum of these waves, and its
spectrum shows how strong each one is:

00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:04.780
bright in the middle, because most of a photo
changes slowly,

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:07.250
with streaks from its strongest edges.

00:01:07.630 --> 00:01:11.285
Now keep only a small disc in the centre, and
switch off the rest.

00:01:11.305 --> 00:01:12.872
What will the photo look like?

00:01:12.892 --> 00:01:14.555
Pause and decide.

00:01:17.315 --> 00:01:18.032
Blurry.

00:01:18.052 --> 00:01:22.082
Only slow waves survive, and a smaller disc
blurs more.

00:01:22.102 --> 00:01:25.620
Keep the outside instead, and only the edges
remain.

00:01:25.640 --> 00:01:28.132
Blurring with a kernel does exactly this:

00:01:28.152 --> 00:01:31.865
convolution in space is multiplication in
frequency.

00:01:32.275 --> 00:01:33.518
One surprise.

00:01:33.538 --> 00:01:36.718
Each wave also has a phase: where its peaks
sit.

00:01:36.738 --> 00:01:39.618
Keep only the phases, and the outlines
survive.

00:01:39.638 --> 00:01:42.500
Keep only the strengths, and the photo is
gone.

00:01:42.970 --> 00:01:45.188
This view runs through vision.

00:01:45.208 --> 00:01:49.200
Anti-aliasing removes fast waves before you
shrink an image,

00:01:49.220 --> 00:01:51.375
as in lesson thirty three.

00:01:51.395 --> 00:01:53.975
JPEG spends its bits on the slow waves.

00:01:53.995 --> 00:01:58.145
And a CNN's first filters often look like
oriented waves.

00:01:58.755 --> 00:02:00.498
So remember three things.

00:02:00.518 --> 00:02:04.372
An image is a sum of waves of different speeds
and directions.

00:02:04.392 --> 00:02:07.685
The centre of the spectrum holds the slow
variation.

00:02:07.705 --> 00:02:11.080
And filtering means turning frequencies up or
down.

